former army major rob shenton served for  12 years before being sent to  afghanistan  but it was his time here that left him  with severe ptsd  after his second deployment in 2012 he  tried to take his own life  for me  by the time 2013 came along that was  enough i i'd had enough and i  generally tried to take my own life  and as a result of that i ended up in a  mental hospital in beijing stoke in a  military ward  and  i knew that my life would never be the  same again  despite his trauma robb says he has no  regrets about what happened in  afghanistan  overall i was proud of what i did there  but now  and when seeing the news  i think myself as obviously a veteran a  former military person i feel somewhat  hopeless because there's nothing that we  can do  to help i think for me personally i went  there and i did my duty and i did my  duties the best of my abilities  other veterans take a harder line and  are angry as they see the taliban regain  control  to watch  today the  the forces the the taliban armed forces  literally going up the streets where  you can see where your friends and  colleagues passed away fighting for  their cause  they went unhanded you know there was no  opposition for them at all  to to go through that and watch them do  that it's quite heartbreaking um  we've fought hard and we've we've  brought a better  existence for the afghan people there  and  and for it to be turned around so quick  and easily it feels like we've been  in total 457 british military personnel  have died in afghanistan  the financial burden on british  taxpayers is estimated to be  approximately 22.2 billion pounds  but veteran support groups say the  hidden cost is on mental health  the images that we're seeing and what's  happening in afghanistan that actually  triggers memories for all of us that  have served there but for some you know  they'll be much more anxious and  not necessarily be processing it as well  as others  so we we've certainly seen more veterans  coming to us and asking for support and  obviously their families as well  with every tragic loss in afghanistan  came a familiar ceremony of death as the  bodies of the fallen were flown home  many veterans and their families are  left questioning was it worth it  charlotte lomas sky news
 

